  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nFriday...Back to the discussion above, will probably have some \nlight showers in portions of the area Friday morning into early \nafternoon, before the wave weakens and drops south allowing omega \nblock to re-erect up the mid and upper MS RVR Valley for a precip \nlull probably into early Sat morning. Similar temp regime to end the \nweek with highs in the 60s to near 70 and lows generally in the 50s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...The latest trends on the medium range \nensembles suggest another short wave trof with a little more punch \nand moisture feed from the south will have a better chance of \nspreading showers acrs the area Saturday and into Saturday night, \npossibly lingering into Sunday morning. Some light to moderate \nrainfall amounts possible in spots this weekend, with clouds and the \nprecip keeping high temps mainly in the 60s with overnight lows in \nthe 50s.\n\nThe next main chance for rain continues to center around \nTuesday/Wednesday of next week. During this time frame the models \nare showing a strong tropical connection to the systems in the \neastern Pacific up into the Midwest. The model consensus already has \na medium to high (50-70%) probability of rain occurring during this \ntime frame. PWATS of 1.5 to 2 inches are being progged by the global \nmodels indicating a high (>80%) probability of heavy rain occurring.\nThis in thanks to a longer range indication that high PWATs will \nlook to reload again next week for that system. This signal would be \nconsistent the with MJO vacillating between phases 8 and 1 which \ncorrelates to above normal precipitation for the area. But there is \nsome variance between the solutions between a large progressive \nlonger wave trof sweeping acrs the mid CONUS, or a slower cut-off \nlow with multiple rounds of rains mid to late week which would be \nthe more troublesome scenario for flooding if it can occur along the \nLat/Lon of the local CWA. Much will change in the modeling, but a \nwindow to watch for next week.",
